          "content": "Example 9.7"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.225,
            0.109,
            0.644,
            0.128
          ],
          "content": "Formula unit mass of calcium nitrate, Ca(NO )"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.225,
            0.129,
            0.613,
            0.147
          ],
          "content": "Atomic mass — Ca = 40 u; N = 14 u; O = 16 u"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.225,
            0.149,
            0.835,
            0.188
          ],
          "content": "Formula unit mass of $\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Ca}(\\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{_2} &= (40 \\mathrm{~u} \\times 1) + \\{(14 \\mathrm{~u} \\times 1) + (16 \\mathrm{~u} \\times 3)\\} \\times 2 \\\\&= 164 \\mathrm{~u}\\end{aligned}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.237,
            0.197,
            0.296,
            0.227
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_18_image_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.302,
            0.203,
            0.492,
            0.222
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Pause and Ponder"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.25,
            0.233,
            0.733,
            0.266
          ],
          "content": "23. Find the formula unit mass of potassium chloride (KCl). Atomic mass — K = 39 u; Cl = 35.5 u."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.25,
            0.27,
            0.793,
            0.305
          ],
          "content": "24. Find the formula unit mass of magnesium hydroxide, Mg(OH) . Atomic mass — Mg = 24 u; O = 16 u; H = 1 u."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.223,
            0.328,
            0.889,
            0.382
          ],
          "content": "Understanding atoms, molecules and chemical bonding reveals the hidden science behind everything we use and consume each day. It shows how tiny atoms combine to build the universe we live in!"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.282,
            0.394,
            0.444,
            0.416
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "At a Glance"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.25,
            0.427,
            0.868,
            0.462
          ],
          "content": "Mass can neither be created nor destroyed in a chemical reaction. This is known as the Law of Conservation of Mass."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.251,
            0.465,
            0.869,
            0.517
          ],
          "content": "A compound always contains the same elements combined in a fixed ratio by mass, no matter how it is formed or from where it is obtained. This is called the Law of Definite Proportions."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.251,
            0.519,
            0.87,
            0.571
          ],
          "content": "A molecule is defined as an electrically neutral entity consisting of more than one atom that can exist independently and shows all its chemical properties."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.251,
            0.573,
            0.868,
            0.625
          ],
          "content": "Atoms combine to form molecules of elements or compounds to become stable. Atoms are held together by a force called a chemical bond."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.251,
            0.628,
            0.868,
            0.662
          ],
          "content": "A covalent bond is formed by the sharing of electrons between atoms."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.082,
            0.662,
            0.178,
            0.888
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_18_image_15.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.251,
            0.665,
            0.868,
            0.717
          ],
          "content": "An ionic bond is formed by the transfer of electrons between atoms, where one atom loses electrons and the other gains electrons to form cations and anions, respectively."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.251,
            0.719,
            0.868,
            0.755
          ],
          "content": "The chemical formula of a covalent compound represents the elements and number of atoms of each element present in it."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.251,
            0.756,
            0.868,
            0.792
          ],
          "content": "The chemical formula of an ionic compound represents the simplest whole number ratio of atoms of different elements present in it."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.252,
            0.794,
            0.869,
            0.829
          ],
          "content": "Molecular mass is the total mass of a molecule, calculated by adding the atomic masses of all the atoms constituting it."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.251,
            0.832,
            0.869,
            0.885
          ],
          "content": "Formula unit mass of an ionic compound is the sum of the atomic masses of all the atoms present in a formula unit (simplest whole number ratio of ions in an ionic compound)."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.075,
            0.942,
            0.114,
            0.96
          ],
          "content": "180"
        },
